y=1, acid labile=1, needle clogging=1, complex manufacture/expensive=1)\n\nWait, let me recount carefully. The rubric says 1 mark each point up to 5 marks total for part (a).\n\nStudent valid points:\n1. Reduce frequency of administration - matches rubric advantage. 1 mark.\n2. Drug degrades in acidic condition (acid labile) - matches rubric disadvantage. 1 mark.\n3. May clog needles - this is a valid disadvantage point (though more explicitly mentioned in part b rubric, it's a real microsphere disadvantage). 1 mark.\n4. Complex manufacture/expensive - matches rubric disadvantage. 1 mark.\n\nThat gives 4 marks for part (a). However, the student mentioned 'drug discharge in stomach is hindered' which is incorrect for an injectable system - this shows some confusion. I'll not penalize but won't award extra marks.\n\nQ6(b) - In situ gelling systems (5 marks):\n\nStudent mentions:\n1. 'injectable polymer+drug+solvent solution which forms solid polymer phase rapidly and degrades over time' - matches 'Systems contain a solvent' and 'drug released by degradation and erosion of the polymer matrix'. Award 1 mark.\n2. 'prepared from poly(lactide-co-glycolide)' - matches 'Generally prepared using a biodegradable polymer'. Award 1 mark.\n3. 'injected as a liquid and rapidly solidifies in tissues. The solvent is safe to inject and is usually n-methyl pyrrolidone (NMP)' - matches 'on administration solvent diffuses away'. Award 1 mark.\n4. 'usually be applied to peptide drug' - this is a specific detail about drug suitability, partially matching 'drug must be soluble with polymer in solvent'. Partial credit - award 0.5, but since marks are whole numbers, I'll consider this as contributing to the overall count.\n5. Clinical benefit question: Student says 'Yes it is more beneficial than microsphere' with reasons including 'ease of administration, improved local bioavailability, reduced dose concentration, improve patient compliance'. However, the rubric states 'No clinical benefit'