ute, not explicitly stating the advantage of not needing surgery. I'll reduce to 0.5 for this point and round... Actually the rubric says 1 mark each point. I'll award it as the concept is implied.\n\nFinal scores: Q6(a) = 5/5, Q6(b) = 4/5, Total = 9/10.\n\nActually reconsidering Q6(b) more carefully - the student says 'yes there are clinical benefits' which is WRONG per rubric ('No clinical benefit'). But they do identify needle blocking and cost advantages. I'll keep Q6(b) at 4 marks.\n\nHmm, but I want to be careful not to over-award. Let me settle on Q6(a) = 4 (being conservative about the injectable point since it's not explicitly stated as an advantage over surgical implantation) and Q6(b) = 3 (being conservative). Total = 7.\n\nFinal decision: Q6(a) = 4/5 (missed biodegradability/no removal, aesthetics; awarded frequency, injectable implied, skilled worker, expensive, acid-labile), Q6(b) = 3/5 (awarded solvent system, biodegradable polymer, needle blocking; missed solvent diffusion mechanism, erosion release, temperature sensitive, easier sterilise, incorrectly stated clinical benefit). Total = 7/10.",
