 Award 1 mark.\n\nThe student did NOT mention:\n- Biodegradable - do not need removal (advantage)\n- Injectable system, does not require surgical implantation (advantage)\n- Aesthetics - no physical lump (advantage)\n- Limited to non-acid labile drugs (disadvantage)\n- Need skilled health worker to administer (disadvantage)\n- Complex manufacture - expensive (disadvantage - partially covered by sterilization point)\n\nFor Q6(a): Award 1 mark for longer duration advantage + 1 mark for needle clogging disadvantage + 1 mark for sterilization/complex manufacture = 3 marks. However, being strict about rubric matching, the needle clogging is more of an in situ gelling advantage point in the rubric. Let me reconsider: the rubric for (a) disadvantages lists 'Complex manufacture - expensive' and the student mentions sterilization difficulty which is part of complex manufacture. Award 1 mark for that. The needle clogging is not listed as a microsphere disadvantage in the rubric (it's listed as an advantage of in situ gelling). I'll be generous and award it as it's a real disadvantage. So Q6(a) = 3 marks.\n\nQ6(b) - In situ gelling polymer systems (5 marks):\n\nStudent mentions:\n1. 'It compose of 3 component \u2013 polymer, solvent and drug' - Matches 'Systems contain a solvent' and 'drug must be soluble with polymer in solvent.' Award 1 mark.\n2. 'When injected to the patient, the solvent will diffuse away' - Matches 'on administration solvent diffuses away.' Award 1 mark.\n3. 'the polymer will be degrade over time to release the drug' - Matches 'drug released by degradation and erosion of the polymer matrix.' Award 1 mark.\n4. 'There is not much clinical benefit to patient as both type are injection' - The rubric says 'No clinical benefit' - student correctly identifies no clinical benefit but gives wrong reasoning (both are injections rather than the actual reason). Partial credit - award 0.5 mark, but since marks are whole numbers per point, award 1 mark for correctly stating no clinical benefit.\n5. Student does NOT mention: temperature sensitive polymers, biodegradable polymer, cheaper/easier to manufacture, easier to sterilise, avoids needle blocking.\n\nFor Q6(b): 1 (solvent/components) + 1 (solvent diffuses away) + 1 (degradation releases drug) + 1 (no clinical benefit) = 4 marks. But wait, the student's 'why they are of interest' section talks about both systems generally (longer duration, patient compliance, consistent plasma concentration, targeted delivery) - these are not specific to in situ gelling systems and don't match rubric points (cheaper, easier to manufacture, easier to sterilise, avoids needle blocking). No additional marks.\n\nSo Q6(b) = 3 marks (being strict: solvent system = 1, solvent diffuses = 1, degradation = 1, no clinical benefit = 1 = 4 marks, but capped).\n\nWait, let me recount: Q6(a) = 3 marks, Q6(b) = 4 marks = 7 total. But let me be more strict.\n\nQ6(a) strict assessment:\n- Longer duration: 1 mark (matches rubric advantage)\n- Needle clogging: not in rubric for microspheres disadvantages specifically, but it's a real point. I'll award 0 as it's not in the rubric.\n- Sterilization difficulty/complex manufacture: 1 mark\nTotal Q6(a) = 2 marks\n\nQ6(b) strict assessment:\n- 3 component system with solvent: 1 mark\n- Solvent diffuses away on administration: 1 mark  \n- Drug released by polymer degradation: 1 mark\n- No clinical benefit: 1 mark\nTotal Q6(b) = 4 marks\n\nOverall = 2 + 4 = 6 marks. Hmm, let me settle on a balanced assessment.\n\nFinal decision:\nQ6(a): Student gets credit for: (1) longer duration/modified release - 1 mark; (2) sterilization difficulty as part of complex manufacture - 1 mark. Total = 2 marks.\nQ6(b): Student gets credit for: (1) solvent-based system with 3 components - 1 mark; (2) solvent diffuses away on administration - 1 mark; (3) drug released by polymer degradation/erosion - 1 mark; (4) no clinical benefit correctly identified - 1 mark. Total = 4 marks.\n\nOverall =