sitive\n2. Generally prepared using a biodegradable polymer\n3. Drug must be soluble with polymer in solvent\n4. On administration solvent diffuses away\n5. Drug released by degradation and erosion of the polymer matrix\n6. No clinical benefit, may be cheaper, easier to manufacture, easier to sterilise\n7. Avoids needle blocking\n\nStudent points:\n1. 'This system consists of poly (lactide-co-glycolide) PLGA polymer, drug and solvent which form into a solution.' - Mentions solvent and polymer. Partially matches point 1 (contains a solvent) and point 3 (drug soluble with polymer in solvent). AWARD 1 mark.\n2. 'n-methyl pyrrolidone (NMP) is mostly to be used as the solvent.' - Specific solvent named, supports point 1. Already counted above.\n3. 'It promotes sustain release of medicine which result in less frequent administration.' - This is a general benefit, not specifically listed in rubric for part (b). NO additional mark.\n4. 'This system is administered via injection. It will solidify within the subcutaneous tissue and slowly release the drug.' - Partially describes the mechanism (solidification = gelling in situ, solvent diffuses away). Matches 'on administration solvent diffuses away' and 'drug released by degradation and erosion'. AWARD 1 mark.\n5. 'The polymers used are degradable and safe as they are natural molecules found within the body.' - Matches 'Generally prepared using a biodegradable polymer'. AWARD 1 mark.\n6. 'It does have a clinical benefit over microspheres as it is a solution and does not consists of small particles. These small particles may aggregate in the needle which is unfavorable.' - Student says there IS a clinical benefit (incorrect per rubric which says 'No clinical benefit') but correctly identifies needle blocking avoidance. The rubric says 'Avoids needle blocking' as a point of interest. AWARD 1 mark for needle blocking point. The clinical benefit assessment is incorrect per rubric.\n7. Easier to manufacture, easier to sterilise, c